1. Speed: acknowledge within 2 seconds, keep the typing indicator, per-attempt timeout 150 seconds (not 20 minutes), try the fastest healthy models first (keep a small success-latency table in ops/backup-manager/latency.json and prefer low latency), race two models in parallel for chat-style questions and take the first reply file, keep long work (queueing tasks, edits) in the background and send the quick answer first, then a follow-up when done. Target: a normal reply in under 60 seconds.

2. One context per burst: collect messages for a short window (about 8 seconds after the last message, and group by media_group_id for albums) into ONE job: all text, all voice transcripts and ALL photos are described first (describe every photo, in parallel), then one prompt contains the ordered list of everything with the image files and descriptions, so the manager understands text and images together. A new message that arrives while a job runs is appended to the same conversation, not run in parallel; if the job already finished it starts the next one. Voice notes are transcribed before the job starts. Test: 3 photos plus a text, and a voice plus a photo.

3. Use every Telegram Bot API capability that helps: send a first short message and EDIT it into the final answer (editMessageText) instead of sending many messages, delete or edit the manager's own messages when it corrects itself, formatted text (HTML, escaped properly), reply-to the owner's message, inline keyboards created by the AI (the AI decides the buttons, callback_data is handled by the listener and routed back to the AI), reactions (setMessageReaction) as quick acknowledgement, sendChatAction, albums (sendMediaGroup), documents and APK sending, polls, message drafts or streaming if available, the commands menu (already set), a better bot description and short description. Research the current Bot API changelog on the web first and list what exists. Put the shared code in scripts/telegram_lib.mjs and scripts/telegram_lib.py with tests, and write plans/TELEGRAM_BOT_KIT.md. Then apply the same kit to the other bots of current projects: SIR AI, AHome (its Telegram code, source only), SIR Jobs, SIR Laptop Manager: each bot's AI can edit its own messages and uses the kit.

4. Bot profile picture: it is low resolution. Make a crisp 640x640 or larger square PNG or JPEG from the SIR AI icon (assets in D:/Projects/SIR AI/assets/brand, or regenerate a high resolution version with ImageMagick or the icon scripts), and set it with the Bot API setMyProfilePhoto if available, otherwise through BotFather in Telegram Web using SIR Autonomy (never press Ctrl+P, close tabs after). Verify with getUserProfilePhotos or a screenshot.

5. Robustness: restart-safe state on disk, the listener survives network drops with retries and backoff (the log shows many "fetch failed"), a watchdog message if the backup manager is stuck for more than 3 minutes, a /status command that shows what it is doing, /handback and /takeover working (the listener once did not write ops/backup-manager.json after /takeover, find out why and fix), and the manager reads ops/tasks reports and verification files each time and uses scripts/taskboard.py to hand out work.

